            <ArticleTitle>Inherited 2q23.1 microdeletions involving the MBD5 locus.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>608-613</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/mgg3.316</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Microdeletions of 2q23.1 disrupting MBD5 and loss of function mutations of MBD5 cause MBD5-Associated Neurodevelopmental disorders (MAND). Nearly all reported patients have been isolated cases of de novo origin.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">This study investigates three families with inherited MBD5 mutations from three different Regional Genetics Centres in the UK.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Two of the parents in the study had MBD5 deletions in a mosaic form. The parent with an MBD5 deletion in an apparently nonmosaic form has a psychiatric disorder in the absence of developmental delay or dysmorphism.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Inherited forms of MBD5 deletions are rare, but do occur, especially in a mosaic form. The phenotypic spectrum of MAND may be wider than previously thought.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
